// spatialfault.nv — the eight ways a call is refused, and why "not
// found" is not one of them.
//
// AN EMPTY ANSWER IS AN ANSWER.  A nearest-neighbour query on an empty
// index, a range query whose area holds nothing, a k-nearest query
// asking for ten hits out of an index of three — none of those is a
// failure.  Each of them has a correct result that the caller can use:
// `None`, an empty list, and three hits.  A library that returns an
// error for any of them makes every caller write a match for a case
// that is not exceptional, and the commonest of them — a query that
// finds nothing — is the one a spatial index is asked most often.
//
// WHAT IS LEFT is the set of calls that describe no index and no
// query: a box whose lower corner is above its upper, a point of a
// different dimension than the index it is being looked up in, a
// negative radius, a node capacity of one.  Every one of them is a
// mistake in the CALL rather than a fact about the data, and every one
// of them is worth finding before the first comparison rather than in
// a wrong answer afterwards.
//
// WHY EVERY VARIANT CARRIES ITS NUMBERS.  A dimension mismatch whose
// message does not print both dimensions sends the reader back to the
// source to work out which of the two arguments was built wrong, and
// the answer is usually that one of them came from a different data
// set than the reader thought.

// Why a spatial call could not be made.
//
// ```novo
// use spatialfault
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let e = SpatialDimensionMismatch(2, 3)
//     println(e.message())
// ```
pub enum SpatialFault
    // A box whose lower bound on some axis is above its upper bound.
    // Carries the axis and the two numbers, because a box built from
    // a swapped pair of coordinates is the usual cause and the axis
    // says which pair.
    SpatialBadBox(axis: Int, lower: Float, upper: Float)
    // A box or a point of a different dimension than the thing it was
    // used with.  Carries what was expected and what arrived.
    SpatialDimensionMismatch(want: Int, got: Int)
    // A box or a point with no coordinates at all.  There is no
    // zero-dimensional space to index.
    SpatialEmptyDimension
    // A node capacity below 2.  A node that holds one entry makes a
    // tree that is a linked list, which is a correct index and a
    // useless one, so it is refused rather than accepted quietly.
    // Carries what was given and the minimum.
    SpatialBadCapacity(given: Int, minimum: Int)
    // A radius query with a negative radius.  Carries it, because a
    // negative radius is almost always a subtraction that came out the
    // wrong way round.
    SpatialNegativeRadius(radius: Float)
    // A k-nearest query with a negative `k`.  Zero is allowed and
    // answers nothing; below zero names no query.
    SpatialNegativeCount(k: Int)
    // An insertion of a handle the index already holds.  A handle is
    // the caller's own index into its own list, so a duplicate means
    // two different boxes claim to be the same item and every query
    // afterwards would be ambiguous.
    SpatialDuplicateHandle(handle: Int)
    // A removal of a handle the index does not hold.  Carries it, so
    // the message can say which one, which is what a caller
    // reconciling its own list against the index needs.
    SpatialHandleNotPresent(handle: Int)

// `SpatialFault` is an `Error`, which is what lets
// `Result<T, SpatialFault>` exist at all (SPEC § 3.4).
impl Error for SpatialFault
    // The one-line message: what about the call was wrong, and the
    // numbers that make it wrong.
    //
    // Panics with `not implemented` until the bodies land.
    fn message(self) -> Str
        todo("spatial-nv.SpatialFault.message")
